 "BUS281": {
  "q": "862904da",
  "a": "Classical management theories include Taylor's scientific management, Fayol's administrative principles and Weber's bureaucracy. They are criticised for treating workers like machines and ignoring motivation, which the Hawthorne studies and the human relations school showed are important.\n\nHowever, they are not outdated. Scientific management is still visible in fast-food chains such as McDonald's and in Amazon warehouses, where tasks are standardised, timed and closely monitored to improve efficiency. Fayol's functions of planning, organising, commanding, coordinating and controlling still describe much of what managers do, and many textbooks are organised around them. Weber's bureaucracy, with clear rules and hierarchy, is still how large public organisations like the NHS and civil service work, because it gives fairness and accountability.\n\nOn the other hand, in knowledge-based and creative organisations, rigid control can reduce innovation. Overall, classical theories are still relevant but need to be combined with later approaches such as contingency theory."
 },
 "ECON0002": {
  "q": "3072a41a",
  "a": "Real GDP per capita measures the value of goods and services produced per person, adjusted for inflation, so it is useful for comparing countries and seeing how living standards change over time. It is widely available and calculated in a similar way across countries, which makes comparisons easier. A country with higher GDP per capita can usually afford better healthcare, education and infrastructure.\n\nHowever, GDP per capita has limitations. It is an average, so it does not show how income is distributed. A country could have high GDP per capita but most of the income goes to a small group of people. It also ignores things like unpaid work, leisure time and pollution, so a country could grow quickly while people are not actually better off. It also does not show how stable the economy is, for example whether growth is followed by a recession.\n\nTo capture inequality we could use the Gini coefficient, and to capture stability we could look at unemployment and inflation. Overall GDP per capita is a good starting point but should be used alongside other measures."
 },
