 "HY116": {
  "q": "a277960e",
  "a": "The July Crisis shows how diplomacy failed. After the assassination of Franz Ferdinand, Germany gave Austria-Hungary the \"blank cheque\", Austria sent Serbia an ultimatum designed to be rejected, and Russia's general mobilisation triggered Germany's Schlieffen Plan, which required invading Belgium and bringing in Britain. Christopher Clark argues that the leaders were \"sleepwalkers\" who made decisions without seeing where they led, which supports the idea that it was a failure of diplomacy.\n\nHowever, long-term structural forces made war more likely. The alliance system turned a local crisis into a European war, and military plans based on fast mobilisation reduced the time for diplomacy. The naval race, imperial rivalry and nationalism in the Balkans also created tension. Fritz Fischer argued Germany had expansionist aims and was willing to risk war.\n\nOverall, structural factors created the conditions for war, but the decisions made in July 1914 turned the crisis into war. Both were necessary, so the claim is too one-sided."
 },
 "LAWS0001": {
  "q": "11eb95ff",
  "a": "Priya's email is an offer to sell for £400. Her promise to keep it open until Friday is not binding because Omar gave no consideration for it (Routledge v Grant), so she could revoke it at any time before acceptance.\n\nOmar's reply \"Would you take £350?\" could be a counter-offer, which would destroy the original offer (Hyde v Wrench). However, it looks more like a request for information about whether she would accept less, as in Stevenson v McLean, so the original offer probably survived.\n\nRevocation must be communicated, but it does not have to come from the offeror. In Dickinson v Dodds the offeree heard from a reliable third party that the property had been sold, and this was enough to revoke the offer. Here Omar learned from a friend on Thursday morning that the bicycle had been sold to Lena, before he emailed his acceptance. If the friend is a reliable source, the offer was revoked before acceptance.\n\nTherefore there is probably no contract between Priya and Omar."
 },
 "PHIL0006": {
  "q": "b6fc8673",
  "a": "Hume argues that reason alone can never motivate action. Reason has two jobs: discovering relations of ideas, as in maths, and discovering matters of fact, such as cause and effect. Neither can produce a desire on its own. Reason can tell me that exercise causes health, but this only motivates me if I already want to be healthy. So passions (desires) are needed, and \"reason is, and ought only to be, the slave of the passions\". Since moral judgements do motivate us, Hume concludes they cannot come from reason alone.\n\nThis view has been developed into the Humean theory of motivation, which says action needs both a belief and a desire. A strength is that it explains why two people with the same beliefs can act differently.\n\nHowever, Kantians argue reason can motivate through recognising the moral law. Nagel argues that prudence shows reasons can motivate without a prior desire: I can be moved by my future interests. Hume could reply that a \"calm passion\" is still involved.\n\nOverall, Hume's argument is persuasive but depends on his narrow view of reason."
 },
 "PHOL0001": {
  "q": "e69f01dd",
  "a": "(a) 0.9% NaCl has about the same osmolarity as the inside of the red cell (about 300 mOsm/L), and Na+ and Cl- do not readily cross the membrane, so it is isotonic: there is no net movement of water and the cell keeps its volume. Pure water has much lower osmolarity, so water moves into the cell by osmosis, the cell swells and eventually bursts (lyses), because red cells have no cell wall.\n\n(b) The Na+/K+-ATPase pumps 3 Na+ out of the cell and 2 K+ in. The cell contains many impermeant anions, such as proteins, which would draw water in. By keeping Na+ effectively outside, the pump balances this, so the cell does not swell. If the pump is blocked, Na+ and water enter and the cell swells.\n\n(c) In the intestine, the Na+-glucose cotransporter (SGLT1) on the apical membrane uses the energy of Na+ moving down its concentration gradient into the cell to carry glucose in against its concentration gradient. The low intracellular Na+ is maintained by the Na+/K+-ATPase on the basolateral membrane, so this is secondary active transport. Glucose then leaves the cell by GLUT2."
 },
 "POLS0006": {
  "q": "f80a4a51",
  "a": "The claim reflects the fact-value distinction: political science, like other sciences, studies how the world is using evidence, while normative political theory asks how it ought to be. Behaviouralists argued political science should be value-free and focus on observable behaviour.\n\nThere is some truth in this. Empirical research can tell us, for example, whether proportional representation increases turnout, but it cannot on its own tell us whether higher turnout is good.\n\nHowever, the distinction is not so clear. Values influence what questions researchers choose and how they define concepts like democracy or power. Empirical findings can also be relevant to normative questions, because \"ought implies can\": if a policy is impossible or has bad effects, that affects whether we should support it. Normative theorists also make empirical assumptions about human nature and institutions.\n\nSo political science cannot by itself decide what ought to be, but it is not completely separate from normative questions and can inform them."
 },
 "PSY215": {
  "q": "77d3e6b8",
  "a": "Deception is used in social psychology to stop participants changing their behaviour because they know the aim of a study (demand characteristics). For example, in Milgram's obedience studies, participants believed they were giving real shocks, and in Asch's conformity studies, the other group members were confederates. Without deception, these studies could not have shown how people really behave.\n\nHowever, deception has ethical costs. It prevents fully informed consent, and it can cause distress, as some of Milgram's participants were very anxious. It may also reduce trust in psychologists. Methodologically, if participants expect to be deceived, they may become suspicious and behave differently, which reduces validity. Repeated deception could make this worse.\n\nThe BPS Code of Ethics allows deception only when there is no alternative and the study is important, with full debriefing afterwards. Alternatives such as role-play have been suggested, but these may not produce realistic behaviour.\n\nSo deception can be justified in some cases, if the scientific value is high and harm is minimised, but it should be a last resort."
 }
